====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive|medical sign}}
## {{senseid|en|cough tr}} ''Sometimes followed by'' '''[[up#Preposition|up]]''': to [[force#Verb|force]] (something) out of the [[lung]]s or [[throat#Noun|throat]] by [[push#Verb|pushing]] [[air#Noun|air]] from the lungs through the [[glottis]] ([[cause#Verb|causing]] a [[short#Adjective|short]], [[explosive#Adjective|explosive]] [[sound#Noun|sound]]), and out through the [[mouth#Noun|mouth]].
##: {{ux|en|Sometimes she '''coughed''' up blood.}}
##* {{RQ:Wodehouse Inimitable Jeeves|page=139|passage=Jeeves '''coughed''' one soft, low, gentle cough like a sheep with a blade of grass stuck in its throat, and then stood gazing serenely at the landscape.}}
## To [[cause#Verb|cause]] (oneself or something) to be in a [[certain#Determiner|certain]] [[condition#Noun|condition]] in the [[manner#Noun|manner]] [[describe]]d in {{senseno|en|cough tr}}.
##: {{ux|en|He almost '''coughed''' himself into a fit.}}
## To [[express#Verb|express]] ([[word#Noun|words]], etc.) in the manner described in {{senseno|en|cough tr}}.
##* {{RQ:Cowper Task|pages=144–145|pageref=144|passage=No ſtationary ſteeds / '''Cough''' their ovvn knell, vvhile heedleſs of the ſound / The ſilent circle fan themſelves, and quake.}}
## {{lb|en|figurative}}
### To [[surrender#Verb|surrender]] ([[information]]); to [[confess]].
### {{lb|en|originally|US|slang}} ''Chiefly followed by'' '''up''': to [[give up]] or [[hand over]] (something); especially, to [[pay up]] ([[money#Noun|money]]).
###* {{RQ:Henry Roads of Destiny|page=324|passage=By the time you get back the men will all be striking out for the fire, and we'll break for the house and collar the dollars. Everybody '''cough''' up what matches he's got.}}
###* {{RQ:Wodehouse Inimitable Jeeves|page=40|passage=Thanks to Jeeves I was not going to be called on to '''cough''' up several thousand quid.}}
###* {{RQ:Wodehouse Summer Lightning|chapter=A Job for Percy Pilbeam|section=II|page=148|passage="Parsloe, will you or will you not '''cough''' up that pig?" / "I have not got your pig."}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}}
## {{senseid|en|cough intr}} To push air from the lungs through the glottis (causing a short, explosive sound) and out through the mouth, usually to [[expel]] something [[block#Verb|blocking]] or [[irritate|irritating]] the [[airway]].
##: {{ux|en|I breathed in a lungful of smoke by mistake, and started to '''cough'''.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Martial|chapter=Epigrammes out of Martial. [To Parthenope.]|translator=Timothe Kendall|title=Flowers of Epigrammes{{nb...|[...] Reprinted from the Original Edition of 1577}}|location=[Manchester]|publisher={{...|Printed [by]}} [Charles Simms] for the Spenser Society|year=1577|year_published=1874|pages=56–57|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=t0RjAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA57|oclc=457858286|passage=Yet notwithſtandyng all this geare, / thou '''cougheſt''' ſtill, perdy / Ye are a craftie knaue, you '''cough''' / to fare deliciouſly.}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Othello Q1|act=IV|scene=ii|page=70|passage=Leave procreants alone, and ſhut the dore, / '''Coffe''', or cry hem, if any body come, {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Scott Canongate 2|volume=III|chapter=X|page=259|passage="Did your lordship's servant see Simon Glover and his daughter?" said Henry, struggling for breath, and '''coughing''', to conceal from the Provost the excess of his agitation.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=Frederic James Post|chapter=A Discourse Touching Rides and Riding|title=Extracts from the Diary and Other Manuscripts of the Late Frederic James Post, of Islington.{{nb...|With a Memoir of His Childhood, Short Life, and Last Illness.}}|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed [by]}} [James Moyes] for private circulation|month=January 23 (date written)|year=1835|year_published=1838|pages=331–332|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=eGYBAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA331|oclc=3471746|passage=But often, when thy face [''i.e.'', that of a horse] is turned ''from'' the stable, thou hast an unaccountable desire to place it in the position occupied by thy tail: thou stoppest, '''coughest''', shyest, and erst, with swift detorsion, turnest round, then, with sidelong glance of my magic caduceus, ominously wagging between the horizon and thy ample sides, I incite thee on, but rarely does thy pace more than trot, ''from'' home.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=A[ngelo] Renzi|chapter=Verbi. Verbes. Verbs.|title=Le polyglotte improvisé, ou l’art d’écrire les langues sans les appendre.{{nb...|Dictionnaire Italien–Français–Anglais, Français–Anglais–Italien, Anglais–Italien–Français, avec 3,000 verbes conjugués. Langue des signes.}}|trans-title=The Improvised Polyglot, or The Art of Writing Languages without Learning Them.{{nb...|Italian–French–English, French–English–Italian, English–Italian–French Dictionary, with 3,000 Conjugated Verbs. Sign Language.}}|location=Paris|publisher=Chez l‘auteur,{{nb...|rue de Madame, 32}}; Chez Baudry,{{nb...|Librarie, rue du Coq-Saint-Honoré, 9}}, et Chez les Principaux Libraries|year=[1840]|page=498|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=AI8M2fs3HXcC&pg=PA498|oclc=1085748399|passage=Tossivi / Tu tossais / Thou '''coughedst'''|brackets=on}}
##* {{RQ:Trollope He Knew|volume=II|chapter=Trevelyan Discourses on Life|page=336|passage=After this he fell a-'''coughing''' violently, and Stanbury thought it better to leave him.}}
##* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=XXXI|page=246|passage="But it is unfortunate—you find me at the moment—" and he stopped short and '''coughed'''.}}
##* {{RQ:Wodehouse Jeeves in the Offing|chapter=XI|passage=I drew a deep breath, and a moment later wished I hadn't, because I drew it while drinking the remains of my gin and tonic. “Does Kipper know of this?“ I said, when I had finished '''coughing'''.}}
## To [[make#Verb|make]] a [[noise#Noun|noise]] like a [[cough#Noun|cough]].
##: {{ux|en|The engine '''coughed''' and sputtered.}}
##* {{RQ:Twain Huckleberry Finn|chapter=XIX|pages=169–170|pageref=169|passage=Wake up, by-and-by, and look to see what done it, and maybe see a steamboat, '''coughing''' along up stream, so far off towards the other side you couldn't tell nothing about her only whether she was stern-wheel or side-wheel; then for about an hour there wouldn't be nothing to hear nor nothing to see—just solid lonesomeness.}}
## {{lb|en|originally|US|slang}} To surrender information; to confess, to [[spill the beans]].

